    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "I'm currently overseeing the acquisition of a new collection of rare 19th-century manuscripts for our university archive here in Belgium. I'm trying to decide whether we should implement the Dewey Decimal Classification (DDC) or move toward the Library of Congress Classification (LCC) for this specific collection. Could you provide a technical comparison of the two systems, specifically regarding their scalability for specialized research collections and their ability to handle granular metadata for historical manuscripts?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The Dewey Decimal Classification (DDC) and Library of Congress Classification (LCC) systems serve different purposes and have distinct strengths. DDC is more general and organized by subject, while LCC is more detailed and organized by library subject division. For a specialized collection of rare 19th-century manuscripts, DDC would be more suitable as it focuses on content cataloging, allowing you to refine and narrow your classification based on more granular metadata. However, LCC is generally preferred in large research libraries due to its granular detail and precise call numbers.",
      "source": "target",
