    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Thanks for that overview. You mentioned that LCC is generally preferred for large research libraries due to its granularity. In our specific case, with these manuscripts potentially being quite disorganized or lacking clear provenance, I'm worried about the \"chaos\" that comes with cataloging items that don't fit neatly into predefined categories. Does one system handle those types of irregular, unorganized archival gaps better than the other?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Both DDC and LCC have some flexibility in handling irregular and irregularly-organized collections, but generally, DDC is more adept at accommodating unclassified or unsorted material. In DDC, irregular objects can be shelved under a combination or \"969\" range, and its flex-scripts notation allows for more nuanced relationships between cataloged items. In contrast, LCC relies heavily on its precise class numbers, which may struggle with unsorted or disorganized materials, but its \"Z-R)\" approximation codes can offer a degree of discretion.",
